---
// The headline result. Every number and every data-dependent claim below is computed from the
// frozen skill_decay.json at build time.
import decayData from "../../../../public/data/surf-forecast/skill_decay.json";
import { ft, listJoin, need, pick, signed } from "../../../lib/surf-forecast/findings";
import { REGION_LABEL, type Region } from "../../../lib/surf-forecast/spots";

const rows = decayData.rows;
const H = "wave_height";
const REGIONS: Region[] = ["hawaii", "norcal", "socal"];
const mae = (scope: string, source: string, lead: number, variable = H) => need(pick(rows, scope, source, variable, lead).mae, "mae");
const skill = (scope: string, source: string, lead: number, variable = H) => need(pick(rows, scope, source, variable, lead).skill, "skill");

const e1 = mae("all", "ecmwf_wam", 1);
const e3 = mae("all", "ecmwf_wam", 3);
const e7 = mae("all", "ecmwf_wam", 7);
const growth = Math.round((e7 / e1 - 1) * 100);
const n0 = mae("all", "ncep_gfswave016", 0);
const d0 = mae("all", "gwam", 0);

// Where does ECMWF a week out beat NOAA's latest run? Decided by the data, not written by hand.
const weekBeatsSameDay = REGIONS.filter((r) => mae(r, "ecmwf_wam", 7) < mae(r, "ncep_gfswave016", 0)).map((r) => REGION_LABEL[r]);
const weekLoses = REGIONS.filter((r) => mae(r, "ecmwf_wam", 7) >= mae(r, "ncep_gfswave016", 0)).map((r) => REGION_LABEL[r]);

const noaa1 = skill("all", "ncep_gfswave016", 1);
const socalEcmwf1 = skill("socal", "ecmwf_wam", 1);
const leads = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7];
const neverBelow = ["ecmwf_wam", "ncep_gfswave016"].every((m) => leads.every((l) => skill("all", m, l) > 0) || leads.slice(1).every((l) => skill("all", m, l) > 0));
const dwdBelowEverywhere = REGIONS.filter((r) => [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6].every((l) => skill(r, "gwam", l) < 0)).map((r) => REGION_LABEL[r]);
const dwdAbove = REGIONS.filter((r) => [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6].every((l) => skill(r, "gwam", l) > 0)).map((r) => REGION_LABEL[r]);

const dirE1 = mae("all", "ecmwf_wam", 1, "wave_direction");
const dirN1 = mae("all", "ncep_gfswave016", 1, "wave_direction");
const dirD1 = mae("all", "gwam", 1, "wave_direction");
const ecmwfDirFirstSkill = leads.find((l) => skill("all", "ecmwf_wam", l, "wave_direction") > 0);
const noaaDirEverPositive = leads.some((l) => skill("all", "ncep_gfswave016", l, "wave_direction") > 0);
---

<section id="skill-decay" class="mx-auto max-w-7xl px-6 py-16 md:px-12 md:py-20">
	<h2 class="font-anton text-4xl md:text-5xl">How fast does a forecast go stale?</h2>
	<div class="mt-3 max-w-[65ch] space-y-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
		<p>
			Slower than you would think. ECMWF's wave height forecast for three days out misses the buoy by {ft(e3)} ft on
			average; its forecast for a week out misses by {ft(e7)} ft. Six extra days of lead time make it only {growth}%
			worse than its one-day forecast. All three systems follow the same nearly flat line.
		</p>
		<p>
			What separates them is where the line starts. Before any forecasting horizon is involved, NOAA's latest run
			already misses by {ft(n0)} ft and DWD's by {ft(d0)} ft. Most of a forecast's error is present on day zero.
			{
				weekBeatsSameDay.length > 0 && (
					<>
						{" "}In {listJoin(weekBeatsSameDay)}, ECMWF's week-ahead forecast is closer to what the buoy recorded than NOAA's
						same-day run.
					</>
				)
			}
			{weekLoses.length > 0 && <> In {listJoin(weekLoses)}, NOAA's latest run is still the better of the two.</>}
		</p>
	</div>

	<div id="fg-skill-decay" class="mt-8">
		<div class="flex flex-wrap items-center justify-between gap-3">
			<div class="flex gap-2" role="group" aria-label="Variable">
				<button type="button" data-fg-variable="wave_height" aria-pressed="true" class="border-2 border-black px-3 py-1.5 font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-wide aria-pressed:border-ink aria-pressed:bg-ink aria-pressed:text-paper">
					Height
				</button>
				<button type="button" data-fg-variable="wave_direction" aria-pressed="false" class="border-2 border-black px-3 py-1.5 font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-wide aria-pressed:border-ink aria-pressed:bg-ink aria-pressed:text-paper">
					Direction
				</button>
			</div>
			<button type="button" data-vg-table-toggle class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-accent-blue" aria-pressed="false">
				View as table
			</button>
		</div>
		<div data-fg-legend class="mt-4 flex flex-wrap gap-x-5 gap-y-2 text-sm text-ink"></div>
		<div data-vg-chart class="mt-2">
			<p class="mt-4 font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-ink-soft">Average miss, in <span data-fg-unit-note>feet</span></p>
			<div data-fg-mae class="overflow-x-auto"></div>
			<p class="mt-10 font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-ink-soft">Skill against persistence</p>
			<div data-fg-skill class="overflow-x-auto"></div>
		</div>
		<div data-vg-table class="mt-2 overflow-x-auto"></div>
	</div>

	<div class="mt-8 max-w-[65ch] space-y-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
		<p>
			The second chart asks the harder question: does the forecast beat assuming the surf a few days from now will look
			exactly like it does today? That is the meteorologist's definition of skill, and it changes the story. One day
			out, persistence is hard to beat. NOAA's day-one skill across all twelve buoys is {signed(noaa1)}, a dead heat,
			and in Southern California even ECMWF manages only {signed(socalEcmwf1)}. The forecasts earn their keep two to
			five days out, once the sea has had time to change and persistence falls apart.
			{neverBelow && <> Within a week, ECMWF and NOAA never fall back below persistence.</>}
			{dwdBelowEverywhere.length > 0 && <> DWD stays below it at every lead in {listJoin(dwdBelowEverywhere)}</>}{dwdAbove.length > 0 && dwdBelowEverywhere.length > 0 ? <>, and above it in {listJoin(dwdAbove)}.</> : dwdBelowEverywhere.length > 0 ? <>.</> : null}
		</p>
		<p>
			Switch to direction and the systems separate the same way: one day out ECMWF is {dirE1.toFixed(0)}° off on
			average, NOAA {dirN1.toFixed(0)}° and DWD {dirD1.toFixed(0)}°.
			{ecmwfDirFirstSkill !== undefined && <> ECMWF only overtakes persistence from {ecmwfDirFirstSkill} days out</>}{noaaDirEverPositive ? <>.</> : <>, and NOAA never does.</>}{" "}
			Part of that is definitional rather than a forecasting failure, as the methodology explains: the buoy reports the
			direction of the dominant wave train, the models a blend of everything in the water.
		</p>
	</div>

	<script src="../../../scripts/surf-forecast/skill-decay-chart.ts"></script>
</section>
